1. System Overview
The application is a React SPA that simulates a financial-crime compliance pipeline for AML/KYC teams. It runs entirely in the browser, with two optional external integrations:


┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  BROWSER (single trust boundary — no server-side logic)             │
│                                                                     │
│  ┌──────────┐   ┌──────────────┐   ┌────────────────┐              │
│  │ mockData │──▶│ riskEngine + │──▶│ Alert / Case / │              │
│  │ ingest() │   │ screening    │   │ SAR / EDD store│              │
│  └──────────┘   └──────────────┘   └───────┬────────┘              │
│                                            │                        │
│  ┌──────────────────────────┐              │                        │
│  │ RBAC (role selector UI)  │──▶ actor     │                        │
│  └──────────────────────────┘   identity  │                        │
│                                            │                        │
│  ┌──────────────────────────┐              │                        │
│  │ auditLog (FNV-1a chain)  │◀─────────────┘                        │
│  └──────────────────────────┘                                       │
└────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                 │ VITE_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY (in-bundle)
                 ├──▶ Anthropic API (SAR + EDD drafting)
                 │
                 │ VITE_SUPABASE_URL + VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY (in-bundle)
                 └──▶ Supabase (alerts, cases, audit_log tables)
Key architectural facts that drive the threat model:

All access control is client-side only. RBAC lives in rbac.ts as a JS object; role selection is a UI dropdown with no server authentication.
API keys ship in the Vite bundle as VITE_* env vars — visible to any user who opens DevTools.
The Anthropic client is called with dangerouslyAllowBrowser: true (aiSarDrafter.ts:47, aiEddDrafter.ts:115).
The audit hash uses FNV-1a — a non-cryptographic, 32-bit hash with trivially exploitable collisions (audit.ts:10).
Supabase RLS is not shown configured — the schema only adds a trigger to block UPDATE/DELETE on audit_log.
2. Trust Boundaries
ID	Boundary	Crossing
TB-1	Browser ↔ Anthropic API	SAR/EDD drafting requests (POST with API key)
TB-2	Browser ↔ Supabase	Persist alerts, cases, audit rows (anon key)
TB-3	Internet ↔ Browser	User loads the SPA; attacker can observe network traffic
