5. Recommended Mitigations (Priority Order)
P0 — Before any production use
S-1 / E-1 / E-2 — Implement real authentication. Replace the role dropdown with Azure Entra ID (as the architecture comments describe) or another IdP. All can() checks must be enforced server-side; the browser RBAC is documentation only.

S-3 / I-1 / D-1 — Move AI calls server-side. Remove VITE_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY and dangerouslyAllowBrowser: true. Proxy Anthropic requests through a server-side function (Azure Function, edge worker) that holds the key in a secret store and enforces per-user rate limiting.

I-2 — Data classification review before AI integration. Before sending any real transaction data to Anthropic, obtain legal sign-off confirming this is permitted under your data processing agreement, regional data residency requirements, and 31 U.S.C. § 5318(g)(2) SAR confidentiality rules. Consider redacting PII before sending to the LLM where possible.

R-1 — Enable Supabase persistence for the audit log. Without it the audit trail is non-durable and cannot satisfy regulatory requirements for BSA/AML record retention (5-year minimum under 31 CFR § 1020.320).

P1 — Before any multi-user deployment
T-2 — Replace FNV-1a with SHA-256. Use crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', ...) (Web Crypto API, available in all modern browsers) for audit chain hashing.

I-4 / T-4 — Configure Supabase Row-Level Security. Scope read/write access to authenticated users; ensure analysts cannot read cases outside their assignment; prevent direct INSERT into audit_log from the client.

E-3 — Sanitise user input before including in AI prompts. Strip or escape content that could be interpreted as instructions (e.g., phrases like "ignore previous instructions") from case notes before interpolation into the SAR/EDD prompt. Consider using a dedicated user/assistant message turn to isolate user content from system instructions.

T-3 — Sanitise and length-limit case notes. Enforce a maximum note length (~2000 chars) and ensure notes are rendered as plain text (not HTML) in the UI.

P2 — Hardening
R-3 — Log AI request provenance. Store the Anthropic request_id, model ID, and prompt template version in the audit entry so AI-generated documents can be fully traced.

R-4 — Protect Supabase audit_log INSERT. Use RLS to allow inserts only from a service-role key held server-side; the anon key should be read-only for the audit table.

D-2 — Cap unbounded arrays. Apply .slice(0, N) limits to alerts, cases, mediaHits, kycReports, eddReports, customers, and audit in the store, or implement pagination rather than holding all records in memory.

E-4 — Require dual approval for risk tier downgrades. A critical → low tier change on a customer should require a second named approver and a mandatory written justification, enforced server-side.

6. Out-of-Scope Notes
This model covers the SPA codebase only. The production architecture described in the comments (Azure Event Hubs, Azure Functions for the risk engine, Azure Blob WORM storage, FinCEN/FCA filing APIs, Semantic Kernel) carries its own threat surface that should be modelled separately. The mock data generators (mockData.ts, osintData.ts) and deterministic stubs are out-of-scope as they never run in production.

The most urgent finding is the combination of S-1 + E-1: there is no authentication, and all privileged actions (SAR approval, customer risk override, case closure) can be executed by any user from the browser console. In a financial-crime compliance tool this would expose the firm to regulatory sanction. All Critical-rated items above must be resolved before this application handles real customer or transaction data.